\f1\fs22 Chanta had spent three hours orchestrating the perfect evening. The view from his penthouse was set to \'93Golden Hour Aurora Australis\'94. Even nature couldn\'92t compete with his wealth anymore. The fourteen-course meal prepared by Executive Chef Rossalind Carpenter sat under warming domes, each dish more obscenely expensive than the last.\
\pard\pardeftab720\fi360\sl220\qj\partightenfactor0
\cf2 <$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93So,\'94 he said, spreading his arms wide as Sarah entered, \'93what do you think? King of the world, baby.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Sarah took in the absurd luxury with a mixture of amusement and unease. Her eyes kept returning to Tony. He looked\'85 extraordinary. Not dressed-up extraordinary. Not \'93new money did a makeover\'94 extraordinary. Just wrong-well. His skin had an almost luminescent quality, his eyes bright and clear, his whole bearing radiating a vitality that didn\'92t match the life he\'92d been living.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>When had Tony become so devastatingly handsome?\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93It\'92s certainly... shiny.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Shiny? This is the apex of civilisation!\'94 Chanta gestured grandly. \'93Voice-activated climate control, a bidet that plays \'91Evolve Me Tender.\'92 I\'92ve got it all.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Fisher glided forward, offering Sarah a drink. \'93Champagne from the Napoleon cellar, madam? It predates even the Dark Ages.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Just water, thanks.\'94 Sarah watched as Chanta commanded various appliances with theatrical hand gestures, each device responding with unnecessary fanfare. The grace in his movements, the effortless charm\'97where had this come from?\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Since when do you have servants?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93They\'92re staff,\'94 Chanta corrected. \'93And they love working here. Right, Fisher?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Indeed, sir. Though I must note that yesterday you referred to us as \'91Brylcreem security,\'92\'94 Fisher replied with artificial cheerfulness.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93That was before I adjusted to my new station in life.\'94 Chanta swept an arm toward the table\'97twenty feet of crystal and linen set up like a diplomatic summit. \'93You know what else changed? Me. I\'92m ready now, Sarah. Ready for... us.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The absurdity of it\'97shouting across a runway of cutlery to have an intimate conversation\'97made Sarah laugh despite herself.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Chanta took that laugh like a favourable market signal and began feeding her course after ridiculous course, narrating his own sophistication between bites.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93And check this out,\'94 he said, standing up as if the apartment required live demonstration. \'93Chanta wants music. Sexy music.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Immediately, an orchestra of the Elvi\'92s most romantic ballads swelled through hidden speakers.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Chanta wants mood lighting.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The lights dimmed to a golden glow, somehow making his hair gleam despite its recent lack of grooming. The same glow caught his cheekbones, and for a moment Sarah had the disorienting sense that he\'92d been polished from the inside.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Chanta wants\'97\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Chanta wants to stop talking in third person?\'94 Sarah suggested.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Chanta laughed, delighted, and for a few minutes it was easy. Too easy. His boyish enthusiasm mixed with his new confidence into something unexpectedly appealing, and Sarah found herself leaning into it, letting the evening carry her along despite the faint, nagging sense that she was being carried by something else.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Eventually, after dinner, they abandoned the table for the living room\'97vast, soft, expensive. Chanta flopped down as if he belonged there by divine right.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93You know,\'94 Sarah said, \'93I think I preferred you as Mr. Fairy Boxers.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Chanta\'92s grin was pure delight. \'93You remember those?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Hard to forget. You were so proud of them, even when you were sleeping in refrigerators.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Now I could buy a thousand pairs. Want to see my collection?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Please tell me you didn\'92t actually\'97\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Framed. In the master suite. Very tasteful.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>This made her laugh again, and suddenly they were closer than they\'92d been in months. Tony caught her hand; his skin was warm and vibrant against hers, his thumb tracing her knuckles with an ease that made her forget to think.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Sarah...\'94 he began, but she was already leaning toward him.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The kiss started tentatively but deepened as months of separation dissolved. When Chanta\'92s hand moved to her waist, she didn\'92t pull away. Instead, she let herself be drawn closer.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93We shouldn\'92t,\'94 she murmured against his lips.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Probably not,\'94 he agreed, continuing the kiss.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>They moved to the plush sofa, Chanta\'92s confidence making the transition smooth. His hands were sure as they began removing her jacket. Sarah responded, matching his intensity as they shed both clothing and inhibitions, even as something inside her kept trying to sit up and clear its throat.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>But as Chanta led her toward the master suite, past walls adorned with priceless art and custom displays, something shifted in Sarah\'92s stomach. Not the queasiness of early pregnancy, but something deeper\'97a revulsion that seemed to emanate from the very air.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>She stopped.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Sarah?\'94 Chanta noticed her hesitation at the threshold of his bedroom.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>She swallowed and looked up\'97\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>A portrait hung just inside. Old oil. Heavy frame. The kind of face that had been taught, from birth, that consequence was something other people experienced. Sarah knew it instantly. The eyes, the mouth, the particular smugness of a man who\'92d built an empire out of other people\'92s lives and called it a legacy.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Her skin crawled.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93I...\'94 She pressed her palm against the doorframe. \'93You have no idea what that man did to me.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93What man? Shakti? If this is about the wedding\'97\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Jamison Hardy!\'94 The name burst from her lips like a confession. \'93That monster, he... I can\'92t...\'94 She swayed slightly, gripping the wall for support.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Sarah, Hardy died like a hundred years ago. What are you talking about?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Horror dawned in her eyes as she realised what she\'92d said. She tried to recover, but her voice shook anyway. \'93I live in his shadow every day, and now so do you.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Chanta reached for her, concerned. \'93Sarah, you\'92re not making sense. Come here, let me\'97\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>But his touch now felt wrong. Not because he was Tony. Because he wasn\'92t, not entirely, anymore. Because everything around him\'97this place, this money, this perfect curated softness\'97felt like it had teeth.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93I changed my mind,\'94 she said abruptly, buttoning her blouse with shaking fingers. \'93This was a mistake. I\'92m feeling ill. I need to leave.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Sarah, wait. What just happened?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93I can\'92t explain it,\'94 she said, backing away from him. \'93I just... I can\'92t be here. I\'92m sorry.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Chanta\'92s expression darkened with hurt, then defensive anger. \'93Fine. You know what? Go back to your husband, your perfect little family. I\'92ll just call up a dozen Elvinas to spend the night with me.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93You\'92ll what?\'94 Sarah\'92s voice went sharp with alarm.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93You heard me,\'94 Chanta said, grabbing his communication device. \'93I\'92ve got numbers for days.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93You\'92ll sleep with them?\'94 Her tone was somewhere between question and accusation, and something else she didn\'92t have a name for.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Chanta chuckled, misunderstanding her concern. \'93Well, not all of them, of course. I\'92m not that virile.\'94 He paused, smirking.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Sarah stared at him, her face draining of colour. \'93You\'92re a monster,\'94 she whispered.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93What? I thought you\'92d approve of sharing the wealth!\'94 Chanta spread his arms, missing her horror entirely. \'93I\'92m helping people. Look at me\'97never felt better. Might live forever at this rate!\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Stop!\'94 Sarah covered her ears, backing toward the door. \'93You don\'92t understand what you\'92re\'97\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93What? Making people happy? Giving them the time of their lives?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>With a choked sob, Sarah fled his penthouse, leaving Chanta standing alone in his perfectly curated paradise, staring at the water glasses and the untouched courses and the view projection.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>He stared at the door for a long moment, as if it might reopen out of sheer appreciation.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Then he picked up his phone again, still frowning, still wounded, still absolutely certain that the problem was timing, or mood lighting, or the bidet playlist.
